seg-5fe6548911da = Worked example: gps-sdr-sim writing a file

# text
seg-eeccc823e109 = This example uses the open-source simulator gps-sdr-sim only to <b>write a baseband file</b> from the exported motion. Kshana does not transmit, and nothing on this page automates replaying that file through a radio. If your laboratory plays such a file into a receiver, that is your operator's responsibility, done only where authorised (cabled or shielded, following the simulator's and the radio's own safety instructions), and Kshana neither documents nor wraps it.

# text
# c1: run1.iq
# c2: tests/round_trip_pack.rs
seg-db81fdadfd19 = Whatever the laboratory then does with <c1/> happens outside Kshana. The receiver's own log, however obtained, is what step { "5" } scores and step { "6" } links. The files and the checks Kshana makes on its own exports are tested on synthetic data only (<c2/>); no laboratory run is claimed here.

# text
seg-dffcbdb8e6c3 = What this method does not do

# text
seg-d0b62c238953 = It does not produce a signal, and it does not say what interference a simulator should generate. The events say when, not what.

# text
seg-3aa11bed60c8 = A single session is a single data point. The result describes that log under that configuration, and says nothing about other receivers, firmware, antennas, or real interference.

# text
seg-b156403456e4 = Heights, rates and time alignment are the operator's to check against the simulator; the export states its own conventions and tolerances and no more.

